<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">
  <PropertyGroup>
    <OutputType>Exe</OutputType>
    <!-- `ssmap`, the name the Makefile and every compile script will use. -->
    <AssemblyName>ssmap</AssemblyName>
    <RootNamespace>SourceSharp.MapCompile</RootNamespace>
    <IsPackable>false</IsPackable>
    <OutputPath>bin\</OutputPath>

    <GenerateDocumentationFile>true</GenerateDocumentationFile>
    <WarningsAsErrors>$(WarningsAsErrors);CS1591</WarningsAsErrors>

    <EnableNETAnalyzers>true</EnableNETAnalyzers>
    <WarningsAsErrors>$(WarningsAsErrors);CA1068;CA2016</WarningsAsErrors>

    <!--
      The runtime settings a LIBRARY must not own (plan_maptools.md 1).

      A library that switches the host process to Server GC has made a decision
      for an ASP.NET service that never asked for it. The exe is the only place
      that legitimately knows it owns the process, so the knobs live here.

      Concurrent GC is OFF deliberately: the background collector exists to keep
      pause times down in an interactive process, and it costs a core doing it.
      A batch compile that is already saturating 32 threads would rather have
      that core and take the pauses, and Phase 5 measures GC pause total, so
      if this is the wrong call it will show up as a number rather than an
      opinion.
    -->
    <ServerGarbageCollection>true</ServerGarbageCollection>
    <ConcurrentGarbageCollection>false</ConcurrentGarbageCollection>
    <TieredPGO>true</TieredPGO>

    <!--
      THERE IS NO TIERING WORKAROUND HERE ANY MORE, AND THE REASON MATTERS.

      `ssmap vvis` used to abort with `Internal CLR error (0x80131506)` in about
      1 run in 13: a fatal ExecutionEngineException with no managed exception
      behind it. A crash report plus a perf map put every frame of the faulting
      stack at `[QuickJitted]`, with the loop method `VisBaseFlow::SimpleFlood`
      on top: 32 workers reaching one OSR patchpoint together. Setting
      `TieredCompilationQuickJitForLoops` false stopped it, and that property
      lived here as a workaround.

      It was NOT a .NET 10.0.12 regression, which is what the earlier note in
      this file claimed. That claim compared Ubuntu's packaged 10.0.12 against
      Microsoft's 10.0.11 and 11.0.0-rc.1, so it varied build origin as well as
      version. Holding the version fixed at 10.0.12 and varying only the build,
      on one machine with one application binary:

          Ubuntu dotnet-runtime-10.0 10.0.12-0ubuntu1~26.04.1   28 / 200
          Microsoft official 10.0.12                             0 / 200

      Narrowed to one file, by dropping a single Ubuntu library into an
      otherwise untouched Microsoft runtime:

          Microsoft 10.0.12, unmodified                          0 / 200
          Microsoft 10.0.12 + Ubuntu libclrjit.so                0 / 200
          Microsoft 10.0.12 + Ubuntu libcoreclr.so              33 / 200

      Same source commit (95017c711e6afc1085133d440e42b4bd78155701) either way,
      so Canonical's build of libcoreclr.so is what breaks, not the JIT and not
      the runtime's source.

      THE REQUIREMENT THIS REPLACES: build and run with Microsoft's runtime,
      which on this machine is ~/.dotnet/dotnet. Ubuntu's /usr/bin/dotnet will
      abort roughly one compile in seven, and no project property here can
      prevent that, because the .csproj does not choose which installed runtime
      build hosts the process.

      Reduced repro and full measurements: /home/lodle/dotnet-osr-repro.
    -->
    <InvariantGlobalization>true</InvariantGlobalization>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!--
      The CLI references the libraries as an ordinary consumer and gets NO
      InternalsVisibleTo. That is what turns "everything the CLI can do, a host
      can do" from an aspiration into a compile error: if a command here needs
      something internal, the fix is to make it public and document it, not to
      open a back door that a third-party host would not have.
    -->
    <ProjectReference Include="..\SourceSharp.MapFormats\SourceSharp.MapFormats.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\SourceSharp.MapTools\SourceSharp.MapTools.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    THE SQLITE PACKAGE IS A HOST REFERENCE; THE GPU PACKAGE ARRIVES BY NAME.

    P12 anomaly 5, root-caused by P15. `ssmap -incremental` never engaged the
    cache on ANY corpus map: the JIT build shipped ssmap.dll with no
    SourceSharp.MapTools.Cache.Sqlite.dll beside it and no deps.json entry for
    it, so HostBackends.Loaded found neither, OpenCacheAsync returned null in
    silence, and `-incremental` was a no-op flag out of the box — on every
    map, cold AND warm: nothing was ever WRITTEN (the cold run itself reported
    `0 cooked, stored 0.0 KB`, and `cache stats` on the same path answered
    "no SQLite backend"; with the package staged beside the binary the same
    corpus warms to 189/189 and 1/1 reuse, so the keys never varied run-to-run
    — the store was simply never opened). t-13's warm-reuse fixture opened the
    store in-process from the test assembly, which project-references the
    package, and its CLI proof ran from a manually staged tree with the dll
    copied by hand; neither posture ever exercised the shipped build output.

    Plan ruling: plan_maptools.md 129 lists the package as OPTIONAL — for the
    LIBRARIES ("the core assemblies reference neither"), and 10a's own wording
    lets the HOST reference the Sqlite assembly. ssmap is the host: a shipped
    `-incremental` that silently cooks everything contradicts the plan's
    mechanism (the SQLite database beside the map, opened by the flag) more
    than a CLI host that names the store it is told to open. MapTools and
    MapFormats stay package-free (LibraryRuleTests,
    CoreMapToolsAssemblyCarriesNoSqliteReference — untouched); MapCompile is
    the CLI, not a core library. The package-absent DEGRADATION stays intact
    for a host that strips or cannot load the package: HostBackends prints
    HostBackends.MissingReason and WithBackendsAsync now says the same on the
    compile path, so a requested store that cannot open is never silent again.

    GPU stays reflection-only (plan 10c): linking it would make Vulkan
    availability a load-time concern of the whole CLI; the honest decline path
    (VRAD0707 + the CPU KD tracer) is tested and stays. Under PublishAot the
    Gpu package is linked statically — NativeAOT has no JIT to bring IL in
    with — and rooted by the rd.xml files named below; HostBackends resolves
    it by name through the default load-context scan. Cache.Sqlite is now a
    plain reference in both postures; the sqlite rd.xml stays as the linker
    root the Activator seam on its store type relies on under trimming.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\SourceSharp.MapTools.Cache.Sqlite\SourceSharp.MapTools.Cache.Sqlite.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <PropertyGroup Condition="'$(PublishAot)' == 'true'">
    <DefineConstants>$(DefineConstants);AOT_LINKED_PACKAGES</DefineConstants>
    <!--
      THE SIX WARNINGS THAT BELONG TO SILK.NET, NOT TO THIS PROJECT.

      All of them are raised inside the statically linked GPU package's own
      dependency chain — Silk.NET.Core.Loader.DefaultPathResolver and
      Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyModel — and every one is the
      single-file-hostile shape those two libraries always had:

        IL3000  DefaultPathResolver cctor + TryLocateNativeAssetFromDeps read
                Assembly.Location to locate native assets. Both sit at the end
                of a fallback chain that returns "not found" when they get
                nothing useful back, which is what happens in a single-file
                app; NativeLibrary then falls through to the app-local probe
                that does find the extracted libvulkan, proven by the aot-smoke
                run (aot-smoke-findings.md quotes the identity/decline lines
                the AOT binary prints through this loader).
        IL3002  the same resolvers read Assembly.CodeBase /
                DependencyContext.Default, both documented to return null
                under single-file and both null-checked by the calling code.

      These cannot be annotated away from here (the source is not ours to
      edit) and there is no per-assembly suppression in the trimmer, so the
      itemised list is the narrowest mechanism the toolchain offers. It is
      gated to PublishAot, i.e. to exactly the posture that links Silk.NET —
      a plain Release build keeps every one of these diagnostics armed, and
      our own assemblies produce none.
    -->
    <NoWarn>$(NoWarn);IL3000;IL3002</NoWarn>
  </PropertyGroup>
  <ItemGroup Condition="'$(PublishAot)' == 'true'">
    <ProjectReference Include="..\SourceSharp.MapTools.Gpu\SourceSharp.MapTools.Gpu.csproj" />
    <RdXmlFile Include="aot-packages-sqlite.rd.xml" />
    <RdXmlFile Include="aot-packages-gpu.rd.xml" />
  </ItemGroup>
</Project>
